RECOMMENDED PRIMARY PROJECT (best balance of feasibility and clinical value)
====================================================================================================================================

  TITLE: "Determinants and consequences of first-line regimen selection between atezolizumab/
          bevacizumab and durvalumab/tremelimumab (STRIDE) in advanced hepatocellular carcinoma"

  DESIGN: two linked aims on the SAME cohort, which is what the data actually support.

   AIM 1 (fully feasible — TIER 2): treatment-selection model.
      Outcome  = regimen chosen (AB vs STRIDE); population = 1L systemic starts 2023-2026.
      N        = 144 AB vs 80 STRIDE overall; 59 vs 40 in the concurrent window with year-SMD 0.03.
      Covariates are measured: ALBI 98%, full liver panel 97%, structured comorbidity 100%,
      pre-index liver imaging 85%, Child-Pugh from notes 68%, ECOG from notes 46%.
      This aim is publishable on its own and quantifies exactly how much of the choice is
      explained by measured liver function, varices/bleeding risk, autoimmune history and
      prior locoregional therapy (PS c-statistic 0.82 — i.e. selection is highly predictable).

   AIM 2 (restricted, hypothesis-generating): survival comparison inside the common-support region.
      Trim to the PS overlap region, which retains 86% of the cohort, then estimate OS and
      time-to-next-treatment with IPTW. Pre-specify that the detectable effect is HR >= 1.66, so
      report this as an estimate with a formal positivity caveat rather than a definitive answer.

  WHY NOT the clinically hottest question (2L after 1L immunotherapy, N7)?
      Because REACH has only 43 TKI vs 51 ICI-based 2L starts after a 1L ICI, falling to 23 vs 30 in a
      concurrent window with a year-SMD of 0.92; the smallest detectable hazard ratio is 2.53.
      That question should be taken to a multi-site collaboration, not attempted here.

